STRUCTURE OF THE CLAIM
  If Q has REAL algebraic coefficients then e^{Q(t)} > 0 on [0,1], so the
  integral is > 0 -- trivial.  The entire content is COMPLEX Q.
  Exactly the shape found for FC in HYP-9076 sec 6: real case trivial by
  positivity, all content over C.

DEGREE 1 IS LINDEMANN-WEIERSTRASS
  Q = a t + b, a != 0:  int_0^1 e^{at+b} dt = e^b (e^a - 1)/a,
  zero  <=>  e^a = 1  <=>  a in 2 pi i Z \ {0}, which is TRANSCENDENTAL.
  So algebraicity of the coefficients is exactly what rules it out.
  (Nonconstant is needed: Q constant gives e^Q != 0 anyway.)

RELATION TO FC -- the precise statement
  int_0^1 e^{Q} dt = sum_m M(Q^m)/m!   with M(t^j) = 1/(j+1).
  FC kills EVERY moment L(f^m) separately (weight w_j = j!).
  The integral conjecture kills ONE weighted sum of moments M(Q^m)
  (weight w_j = 1/(j+1)).  Same functional shape, different demand.

THM-3022 APPLIED TO THE [0,1] LEBESGUE WEIGHT  w_j = 1/(j+1)
  log-convex (w_j^2 < w_(j-1) w_(j+1) for j=1..39): True
  Q_w(a,b) > 0 for all 0<=a<b<=9: True   violations: []
  e.g. Q_w(0,1) = 1/12,  Q_w(1,3) = 11/1680,  Q_w(2,5) = 7/3960

  => by THM-3022 the two-slot threshold for the [0,1] Lebesgue weight is 2:
     M(f) = M(f^2) = 0 already forces f = 0.  So the FC-analogue for this
     measure is settled at two slots by the same log-convexity argument.
